WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.130
Film Courage: Clifton, when you first started
acting and booking roles, what was your vision

00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:05.780
for your career?

00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:10.610
Clifton Collins, Jr.: Vision for my acting
career?

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:16.800
Gosh, I think when you’re younger…I don’t
know if we always know the real reasons why

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:19.340
we get into a business.

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:22.270
Sometimes it’s in hindsight that you realize
“Oh, these were the steps that led me up

00:00:22.270 --> 00:00:23.779
to this?”

00:00:23.779 --> 00:00:29.020
I don’t know that I had one?

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:31.910
I know that I wanted to do roles that mattered.

00:00:31.910 --> 00:00:38.350
I wanted to represent communities of people
that didn’t have voices, I knew that going

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:39.350
in.

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:44.770
It’s kind of interesting insight to have
when I was 17.

00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:48.920
Film Courage: You grew up around the business,
didn’t you?

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:49.920
Your grandfather was in the business?

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:53.910
Clifton: I’m a fourth generation entertainer
so my Grandfather, my Great-Grandfather, my

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:54.910
Great-Grandmother.

00:00:54.910 --> 00:01:00.449
My Grandfather was a contract player for John
Wayne, he did a lot of Westerns.&nbsp;

00:01:00.449 --> 00:01:08.299
Film Courage: So when you told your family
that you wanted to be an entertainer, did

00:01:08.299 --> 00:01:13.290
they say…well there is also a downside to
it?

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:15.350
Were they always positive about it or were
they cautious?

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:19.359
Clifton: My Grandfather was the only positive
voice.

00:01:19.359 --> 00:01:23.319
He was the only “You can do it” that I
got.

00:01:23.319 --> 00:01:28.029
My Mom was not for my career, many friends
weren’t.

00:01:28.029 --> 00:01:29.579
It’s a tough business.

00:01:29.579 --> 00:01:32.414
But my Grandfather was like “No, you can
do it.

00:01:32.414 --> 00:01:33.414
I know you can do it.”

00:01:33.414 --> 00:01:36.950
And I just needed to hear it, I just needed
someone to believe in me.

00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:38.829
And that was the one voice.

00:01:38.829 --> 00:01:41.700
Film Courage: Did you do a lot of imitations
growing up?

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:45.679
Because you’re like a chameleon-like style,
you can be so many different characters.

00:01:45.679 --> 00:01:47.083
Did you do imitate a lot of people growing
up, teachers, etc.?

00:01:47.083 --> 00:01:50.079
Clifton: I did, that’s really intuitive
of you.

00:01:50.079 --> 00:01:51.109
I did.

00:01:51.109 --> 00:01:53.609
I got in a lot of trouble in high school because
of it.

00:01:53.609 --> 00:02:01.109
I got laughs, I did…because Grandpa…you
think of a movie like RIO BRAVO with Dean

00:02:01.109 --> 00:02:09.329
Martin and Walter Brennan and all these unique
different voices, it was just fun to imitate.

00:02:09.329 --> 00:02:14.459
We started tap dancing when we were seven
so sometimes just to pass the time as a kid

00:02:14.459 --> 00:02:18.080
I’d do voices.

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:21.159
Me and my cousin, I mean you hate it…when
you’re tap dancing when you’re seven,

00:02:21.159 --> 00:02:22.569
you just don’t like it.

00:02:22.569 --> 00:02:24.060
Today, I love it.&nbsp;

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:28.620
Film Courage: What do you consider as your
first success as an actor?

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:31.299
Clifton: First success, gosh?

00:02:31.299 --> 00:02:39.269
Honestly, I think there’s a danger in saying
a first success because I think life is a

00:02:39.269 --> 00:02:41.909
series of successes and failures.

00:02:41.909 --> 00:02:51.239
So even in this business success to me is
doing the job that I got to do in that room,

00:02:51.239 --> 00:02:57.090
whether I get the role or not but the fact
that I’m able to pull off a performance

00:02:57.090 --> 00:03:02.050
and give a performance because I like to give
a performance when I audition instead of auditioning.

00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:05.730
You know that kind of like to do a lot of
the prep work prior, like some of the heavier

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:09.640
prep work that most actors reserve for after
you get the gig.

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:15.680
If I have the time, I’ll delve completely
into it, it’s just part of the fun if you

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:17.239
want to do a good job.

00:03:17.239 --> 00:03:23.030
But in regards to making a living, it’s
a struggle.

00:03:23.030 --> 00:03:29.390
You’re juggling jobs, you’re juggling
regular jobs that let you go to auditions

00:03:29.390 --> 00:03:31.840
on top of auditioning.

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:35.970
So there’s a struggle and success in that
as well.

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:41.150
I’d say in terms of benchmarks in my career
like the first gig that you get where you

00:03:41.150 --> 00:03:43.859
get to be SAG, you get your SAG card you’re
like “Wow!

00:03:43.859 --> 00:03:45.480
I finally got my SAG card.”

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:49.760
And that was my first commercial so that was
fun.

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:56.889
So then you look at a film like maybe 187
which was probably one of the greater challenges

00:03:56.889 --> 00:03:58.219
in my early career.

00:03:58.219 --> 00:04:05.220
There was so much to learn, the mentorship
I got from Sam Jackson was just kind of set

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:07.370
the pace for me in a lot of different ways.

00:04:07.370 --> 00:04:12.889
In the ways that he acted and how he prepped
even deeper, harder and longer.

00:04:12.889 --> 00:04:14.000
I was like “Oh my God.

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:15.629
I’ve got to do this.”

00:04:15.629 --> 00:04:21.609
Because I want to be that good watching Sam
do his thing and I was just “My God?”

00:04:21.609 --> 00:04:25.551
So he set the bar pretty high for me so that
was fun.

00:04:25.551 --> 00:04:31.620
But I think my first really big role to be
challenged by somebody of Sam’s caliber

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:36.340
and to rise up to that occasion, that was
fun.

00:04:36.340 --> 00:04:42.639
Film Courage: I know with a lot of things
in this town you can go to one level and you

00:04:42.639 --> 00:04:44.480
think “Oh, it’s just going to keep being
like this.”

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:48.470
It’s going to be this upward staircase and
sometimes you realize you’re back to square

00:04:48.470 --> 00:04:49.470
one.

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:50.470
Clifton: Sure.

00:04:50.470 --> 00:04:52.050
Film Courage: In any endeavor out here.

00:04:52.050 --> 00:04:54.389
Clifton: Sure, I’m very familiar with square
one.

00:04:54.389 --> 00:04:56.960
Film Courage: So am I.

00:04:56.960 --> 00:04:59.280
How do you deal with that square one point?

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:00.280
Clifton: You just love it.

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:01.280
Film Courage: You do?

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:02.280
Clifton: You have to.

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:07.199
You’ve got to love the process.

00:05:07.199 --> 00:05:15.400
You’ve got to be okay with square one.

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:19.120
It’s always a struggle I think.

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:27.620
Different forms of challenges reinvent themselves,
obstacles, you try to develop a foresight

00:05:27.620 --> 00:05:29.680
to see those things coming.&nbsp;

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:37.139
But to get back to square one, you embrace
it and you work diligently and extra hard

00:05:37.139 --> 00:05:45.770
to get ahead in your craft and auditions or
whatever it is that you’re doing, writing,

00:05:45.770 --> 00:05:46.770
directing.

00:05:46.770 --> 00:05:51.819
Film Courage: So who is your character Nathan
in A CROOKED SOMEBODY?

00:05:51.819 --> 00:05:59.460
Clifton: Nathan is a troubled soul who has
been dealing with his past and as a result

00:05:59.460 --> 00:06:03.539
he is struggling with atonement.

00:06:03.539 --> 00:06:06.960
He wants to atone I think he just doesn’t
know how.

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:12.610
Yeah, that’s a difficult question.

00:06:12.610 --> 00:06:16.759
Film Courage: Do you think people can change?

00:06:16.759 --> 00:06:17.759
Sorry to jump in but…

00:06:17.759 --> 00:06:18.759
Clifton: No, please.

00:06:18.759 --> 00:06:20.920
Film Courage: Do you think people really can
change?

00:06:20.920 --> 00:06:29.509
Clifton: I do believe that people can change
but also you’ve got to go past square one.

00:06:29.509 --> 00:06:36.800
You’ve got to get knocked down and be humbled,
like truly, truly your soul’s got to be

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:37.800
humbled.

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:41.870
And I think only then will real change happen.

00:06:41.870 --> 00:06:51.949
I don’t think…it’s just amazing in these
times as we look at our society, I find it

00:06:51.949 --> 00:06:59.080
troubling that people have to get knocked
down to have any sense of empathy or compassion

00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:00.080
for others.

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:04.470
It’s like “Why do you have to be down
and out to care about people.”

00:07:04.470 --> 00:07:08.030
You know what I mean?

00:07:08.030 --> 00:07:15.340
So Nathan is already down and out, I think
he’s probably been down and out for quite

00:07:15.340 --> 00:07:16.340
some time.

00:07:16.340 --> 00:07:23.139
That beats you down when you don’t address
whether it’s a psychical ailment, emotional,

00:07:23.139 --> 00:07:28.500
mental, things like that they are going to
turn into something else that’s not positive

00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:36.290
but continue to grow and attempt to manage
it as best as you can and survive.&nbsp;

00:07:36.290 --> 00:07:42.490
It didn’t really try to find out what Nathan
used to do but I tried to reverse engineer

00:07:42.490 --> 00:07:51.699
so much stuff so he still lives in a trailer
in an abandoned area so he’s surviving barely.

00:07:51.699 --> 00:07:54.550
Film Courage: He might be protecting himself,
too.

00:07:54.550 --> 00:07:58.439
Clifton: Could be, there’s a lot of different
things happening.

00:07:58.439 --> 00:08:10.491
But certainly I wanted to be able to demonstrate
that darkness is in all of us and it’s up

00:08:10.491 --> 00:08:17.009
to us to act on it and I wanted to be able
to show that he’s quite capable of being

00:08:17.009 --> 00:08:18.050
that bad person.

00:08:18.050 --> 00:08:30.500
It’s really about him being in control of
his emotions and a desire to really change.

00:08:30.500 --> 00:08:35.900
I don’t know…I think we slide the scales
of justice, which isn’t really justice when

00:08:35.900 --> 00:08:42.720
you slide them and you make excuses for certain
things that you do.

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:46.680
You can look at the good that you do and go
“Oh, I’ve done these good things.”

00:08:46.680 --> 00:08:51.410
But I’ll turn a blind eye to these bad things
that I do occasionally.

00:08:51.410 --> 00:08:53.500
You’re either bad or you’re good.

00:08:53.500 --> 00:08:57.910
Film Courage: Yes and without giving away
too much of the story and as Trevor (The Producer/Director

00:08:57.910 --> 00:09:02.800
of A CROOKED SOMEBODY) these character arcs
you can also see what seems good, it’s an

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:10.280
interesting look at perception (we’ll just
keep it at that) and looking deeper at someone’s

00:09:10.280 --> 00:09:18.130
intentions which is something that we’re
exposed to every day on Twitter without going

00:09:18.130 --> 00:09:19.130
too far into it.

00:09:19.130 --> 00:09:20.160
Clifton: On my God.

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:24.880
Film Courage: How often would you say you’re
handed an acting role?

00:09:24.880 --> 00:09:29.000
Clifton: Like get offers?

00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:34.500
Film Courage: Yes, where someone is like “This
is a role we know we want you to play.”

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:40.950
Versus you have to go in and audition, you
have to play the game versus someone saying

00:09:40.950 --> 00:09:42.570
we know we want you specifically.

00:09:42.570 --> 00:09:47.970
Clifton: Well you don’t want to do every
offer that you get.

00:09:47.970 --> 00:09:50.400
So…how often?

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:52.300
It’s kind of cyclical.

00:09:52.300 --> 00:09:55.870
I think it kind of depends what’s out, what’s
popular.

00:09:55.870 --> 00:10:02.810
I’ve got so much work behind me there is
no telling who has seen what.

00:10:02.810 --> 00:10:13.970
So it goes in waves, I think in the last few
years this was certainly one of them [Clifton’s

00:10:13.970 --> 00:10:14.970
role of Nathan in A CROOKED SOMEBODY].

00:10:14.970 --> 00:10:17.780
But when I read the script I was like “Oh
my God I really want to play this role.

00:10:17.780 --> 00:10:18.780
This is amazing.”

00:10:18.780 --> 00:10:26.370
I guess we found each other, you know what
I mean?

00:10:26.370 --> 00:10:31.080
And I loved working with Trevor and Rich of
course and it was just great.

00:10:31.080 --> 00:10:35.000
The collaboration was really fantastic, so
it was a good time.

00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:37.150
And these kind of Indies are special.

00:10:37.150 --> 00:10:47.000
They’re open to suggestions and ideas based
off of some of the research that I’d done.&nbsp;

00:10:47.000 --> 00:10:54.970
But to answer your question thought I probably
get an offer or a couple offers a month.

00:10:54.970 --> 00:10:59.310
Film Courage: So it that what determines it
for you because I thought with Nathan you

00:10:59.310 --> 00:11:04.490
really owned his this person, you inhabited
this person’s skin.

00:11:04.490 --> 00:11:09.190
Is that why you might turn something down
is that you feel I don’t know if I can own

00:11:09.190 --> 00:11:10.190
it?

00:11:10.190 --> 00:11:11.190
I don’t know if I can respect it?

00:11:11.190 --> 00:11:22.000
Clifton: Well I think I definitely appreciate
a challenge, a challenge as an artist.

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:26.770
I like to be challenged to do things that
I haven’t done before so I get to learn

00:11:26.770 --> 00:11:35.040
a new skill set or I get to jump into another
culture, another lifestyle.

00:11:35.040 --> 00:11:40.880
There’s only a handful of roles that I won’t
really play like pedophiles.

00:11:40.880 --> 00:11:46.620
The one they called me about THE LOVELY BONES,
I was like “I heard about this book.

00:11:46.620 --> 00:11:48.680
It’s an amazing book from what I understand.

00:11:48.680 --> 00:11:52.870
And “Yeah, we want to meet with you about
this role.”

00:11:52.870 --> 00:11:59.550
“It’s about” “Well it’s not really
about a little girl and?”

00:11:59.550 --> 00:12:02.200
“Well you don’t really him do things.”

00:12:02.200 --> 00:12:03.450
“It doesn’t matter.”

00:12:03.450 --> 00:12:09.260
I don’t want to be the image of someone
who says “Wow, that’s what I do.”

00:12:09.260 --> 00:12:12.450
Or “That’s how I feel.”

00:12:12.450 --> 00:12:14.430
I just can’t, I can’t.

00:12:14.430 --> 00:12:18.020
The research on that is taxing on the soul.

00:12:18.020 --> 00:12:24.690
I’m very ani-that…so.&nbsp;

00:12:24.690 --> 00:12:26.990
So I passed on it.

00:12:26.990 --> 00:12:31.610
I said “Hey let Stanley Tucci get it and
get a nomination and that was before his name

00:12:31.610 --> 00:12:32.610
was even in the mix.

00:12:32.610 --> 00:12:38.400
And if I’m not mistaken I think Stanley
did do it and get a nomination.

00:12:38.400 --> 00:12:39.830
I don’t know.

00:12:39.830 --> 00:12:41.740
I’d be ashamed personally.

00:12:41.740 --> 00:12:44.340
But that’s just me, you know?

00:12:44.340 --> 00:12:46.310
Film Courage: Sure.

00:12:46.310 --> 00:12:51.010
If you were do give someone a five minute
acting lesson, what would it be a couple things?

00:12:51.010 --> 00:12:56.540
Clifton: Five minute acting lesson?

00:12:56.540 --> 00:13:04.480
Gosh I guess it depends on the material because
you can’t teach everything, especially the

00:13:04.480 --> 00:13:05.860
stuff that you learn in 30 years.

00:13:05.860 --> 00:13:13.140
So I’d rather give you what you need for
this audition than what you will carry throughout

00:13:13.140 --> 00:13:14.520
your career.

00:13:14.520 --> 00:13:18.420
Hopefully with those little lessons will be
someone creative anchor to keep in your back

00:13:18.420 --> 00:13:20.290
pocket which is always sweet.

00:13:20.290 --> 00:13:29.570
I’ve ran into a few older…older than when
I worked with them kids that reached out to

00:13:29.570 --> 00:13:34.960
me recently and thanked them for the acting
lessons that I taught them and the mentorships.

00:13:34.960 --> 00:13:41.580
Film Courage: So we know that being on set
is not just acting, it’s navigating personalities

00:13:41.580 --> 00:13:44.360
and politics and hierarchies.

00:13:44.360 --> 00:13:47.470
How do you earn the respect of a fellow actor?

00:13:47.470 --> 00:13:53.710
Clifton: Do the work, do the work, do the
work.

00:13:53.710 --> 00:14:00.420
And it’s funny because you’ll do certain
projects with an Ed Harris and then on this

00:14:00.420 --> 00:14:05.960
film [A CROOKED SOMEBODY] but I already won
Ed’s respect with this film and on WESTWORLD.

00:14:05.960 --> 00:14:11.920
And so I called a few friends that had worked
with him to find out a little background on

00:14:11.920 --> 00:14:12.920
him.

00:14:12.920 --> 00:14:19.320
And I studied really hard to learn my lines,
I studied really hard to learn his lines just

00:14:19.320 --> 00:14:20.930
in case he says “What’s my line?”

00:14:20.930 --> 00:14:23.750
And I could be like “I think you say….maybe?”

00:14:23.750 --> 00:14:28.240
You never know how someone is going to take
it.

00:14:28.240 --> 00:14:29.330
You have to navigate.

00:14:29.330 --> 00:14:35.300
So I navigate and he goes “Oh?

00:14:35.300 --> 00:14:36.930
Thanks partner.”

00:14:36.930 --> 00:14:44.310
So I did that and then I slammed the scene
where my wife goes and then he came running

00:14:44.310 --> 00:14:48.320
over to me pointing at me and he was like
“Yes!

00:14:48.320 --> 00:14:49.320
Yes!

00:14:49.320 --> 00:14:50.320
Yes!”

00:14:50.320 --> 00:14:54.210
Pointing and frothing and he frickin’ kicks
me in my shin real hard and he goes “That’s

00:14:54.210 --> 00:14:55.690
how you do it!”&nbsp;

00:14:55.690 --> 00:14:59.240
And then when he walked away I go “I hope
that’s going to leave a mark.

00:14:59.240 --> 00:15:03.500
Look what Ed Harris did to me because I did
a good job!”&nbsp;

00:15:03.500 --> 00:15:04.970
It’s pretty rad.

00:15:04.970 --> 00:15:06.440
It’s pretty awesome.

00:15:06.440 --> 00:15:08.710
So that’s how I got his respect.

00:15:08.710 --> 00:15:11.930
Film Courage: But I’m sure…like you said
you have to navigate.

00:15:11.930 --> 00:15:17.630
Clifton: You’ve got to navigate, Sam Jackson
I was dying to get his respect.

00:15:17.630 --> 00:15:23.410
He’s definitely a great mentor only second
to my grandfather.

00:15:23.410 --> 00:15:27.690
But this man knows everybody’s, he knows
everybody’s lines.

00:15:27.690 --> 00:15:32.740
He knows when the camera moved and on what
line and where he did what.

00:15:32.740 --> 00:15:33.740
He knows everything.

00:15:33.740 --> 00:15:35.470
It’s really amazing.&nbsp;

00:15:35.470 --> 00:15:44.980
So in rehearsals…he was teaching me a lessons,
we’ve had this conversation before, he’s

00:15:44.980 --> 00:15:49.610
got these monologues at the end of the film
that are actually much bigger than what ended

00:15:49.610 --> 00:15:50.610
up in the film.

00:15:50.610 --> 00:15:57.040
And in rehearsals Kevin Reynolds is like “Hey,
you guys ready to do this scene?”

00:15:57.040 --> 00:15:59.760
And Sam looks up and says “Yeah, man, let’s
do it?”

00:15:59.760 --> 00:16:01.970
And he throws the scenes on the floor.

00:16:01.970 --> 00:16:07.540
Sam threw them out and he doesn’t need them
(he’s got the big speeches anyway).

00:16:07.540 --> 00:16:11.800
So then I’m holding my gun and he looks
up at me with tears (he can get tears from

00:16:11.800 --> 00:16:13.450
this eye and this eye by the way.

00:16:13.450 --> 00:16:18.310
He can make this cry right now, he can hold
them, I can’t do that.

00:16:18.310 --> 00:16:26.140
But he can so when I saw him holding the tears
he literally put the pedal to the medal.

00:16:26.140 --> 00:16:27.780
And I thought “Oh, my God.

00:16:27.780 --> 00:16:31.300
I don’t know if I can ever be that good?”

00:16:31.300 --> 00:16:41.390
So I just remember racing home and delving
as deeply as I could just to get any respect

00:16:41.390 --> 00:16:42.550
from him.

00:16:42.550 --> 00:16:47.190
He’s a tough love kind of guy, so you’ve
got to really, really earn it.

00:16:47.190 --> 00:16:52.720
Film Courage: Some times those kind of people
are easier to trust.

00:16:52.720 --> 00:17:00.100
In an industry where…you want to be professional
but tough love people are easier to trust

00:17:00.100 --> 00:17:02.480
because you know where they’re coming from.

00:17:02.480 --> 00:17:11.380
Clifton: I talk to him quite often…I honestly
really didn’t know, I think this came…I

00:17:11.380 --> 00:17:21.871
lost my father, I was at my father’s funeral
two days before the climax of the movie, we

00:17:21.871 --> 00:17:28.250
shot the movie in sequence so I was really
emotional…It was a rather suicidal-type

00:17:28.250 --> 00:17:31.210
of scenario.

00:17:31.210 --> 00:17:38.340
We’d done the rehearsal and they asked me
if I minded doing my coverage first instead

00:17:38.340 --> 00:17:43.010
of Samuel because I was obviously dealing
with some heavy life issues.

00:17:43.010 --> 00:17:49.130
They all supported me tremendously and I think
it was then that I had the courage to ask

00:17:49.130 --> 00:17:53.309
“Well, what’s Sam think about the work
I’m doing anyway?”

00:17:53.309 --> 00:17:57.490
“Sure they can do coverage on me first,
I’m fine with that.

00:17:57.490 --> 00:17:58.490
Is Sam cool with that?

00:17:58.490 --> 00:17:59.490
Is that what Sam wants?

00:17:59.490 --> 00:18:01.740
Because I’ll do whatever Sam wants.”

00:18:01.740 --> 00:18:03.340
And they were like “Yeah, he’s totally
fine with it.”

00:18:03.340 --> 00:18:07.940
Because that’s when I asked them because
Samuel himself didn’t tell me, he was holding

00:18:07.940 --> 00:18:09.110
it close to the chest.

00:18:09.110 --> 00:18:12.289
He’s really dope, I mean I love him so…

00:18:12.289 --> 00:18:19.230
Film Courage: It’s interesting because sometimes
you’re at a point where certain things are

00:18:19.230 --> 00:18:25.669
going on and if everything falls apart, it’s
okay (we were having this conversation driving

00:18:25.669 --> 00:18:32.759
over here and I’m stealing your words) but
as long as you’re staying true to yourself

00:18:32.759 --> 00:18:37.480
and some amazing things can happen because
you’re at this point where you’re not

00:18:37.480 --> 00:18:42.390
really about pleasing somebody, you’re all
about doing what’s right in the moment and

00:18:42.390 --> 00:18:44.340
sometimes some really amazing things can happen.

00:18:44.340 --> 00:18:49.370
Clifton: That’s a good way to live.

00:18:49.370 --> 00:18:56.779
Because as true as we think we’re being
with ourselves we can always be a little truer,

00:18:56.779 --> 00:19:03.080
seeking a depth of authenticity if you will.

00:19:03.080 --> 00:19:10.889
I’ve just been on this crazy journey lately
of my work and the demands of trying to do

00:19:10.889 --> 00:19:18.659
better work has pushed me to lose sleep and
study harder and faster and just go longer

00:19:18.659 --> 00:19:24.750
and so it’s interesting because if you pay
attention to the signs in those moments and

00:19:24.750 --> 00:19:32.740
do what’s right and what speaks to your
integrity, I can always rest well with whatever

00:19:32.740 --> 00:19:33.740
happens.

00:19:33.740 --> 00:19:37.850
I’m not feeling bad, I’m feeling good
about the decisions that I make and that’s

00:19:37.850 --> 00:19:39.070
a good feeling.

00:19:39.070 --> 00:19:41.570
Film Courage: What movie made you want to
be an actor?

00:19:41.570 --> 00:19:45.830
Clifton: I can’t really say it was a movie.

00:19:45.830 --> 00:19:55.290
It was actually a series of…you go to college
and they’re like “Oh, you’ve got to

00:19:55.290 --> 00:20:00.090
select what you’re going to do for the rest
of your life.”

00:20:00.090 --> 00:20:01.090
And I’m like “At 18?

00:20:01.090 --> 00:20:02.090
I can’t even buy alcohol.

00:20:02.090 --> 00:20:04.231
You want me to make a life decision?”

00:20:04.231 --> 00:20:05.231
You know what I mean?

00:20:05.231 --> 00:20:06.231
So it’s just such a weird thing.&nbsp;

00:20:06.231 --> 00:20:12.370
I went to a college prep high school so a
lot of these guys were going to Ivy League

00:20:12.370 --> 00:20:13.370
colleges afterwards.

00:20:13.370 --> 00:20:18.910
I figured out toward the end of high school
that I didn’t want to do that, that I wanted

00:20:18.910 --> 00:20:20.230
to be an actor.&nbsp;

00:20:20.230 --> 00:20:29.220
And during this moment of wondering should
I do it?

00:20:29.220 --> 00:20:36.720
I was teaching martial arts at the time to
children and I had a moment where I just had

00:20:36.720 --> 00:20:43.990
a series of adults and children ask me if
I was an actor, or that I reminded them of

00:20:43.990 --> 00:20:50.320
this actor or this character, all within like
six to eight week span.

00:20:50.320 --> 00:20:53.659
I’d have people with me when it would be
happening, it was just happening so frequently

00:20:53.659 --> 00:20:59.159
and it was like “Alright, maybe I should
listen to the voices of these people that

00:20:59.159 --> 00:21:00.480
I don’t know.

00:21:00.480 --> 00:21:05.140
And I asked my grandfather, Mom wasn’t into
it, she’s like “You’re not going to

00:21:05.140 --> 00:21:06.140
make it.

00:21:06.140 --> 00:21:07.140
No way in hell.”

00:21:07.140 --> 00:21:08.380
She actually laughed at me.

00:21:08.380 --> 00:21:09.549
Which sucks as a kid.

00:21:09.549 --> 00:21:15.429
You turn to your parents as mentors and teachers
and this and that but that’s a whole other

00:21:15.429 --> 00:21:16.429
story.

00:21:16.429 --> 00:21:17.429
Grandpa said yes.

00:21:17.429 --> 00:21:18.429
So I’m grateful for that.

00:21:18.429 --> 00:21:19.539
When he said “You can do it."

00:21:19.539 --> 00:21:23.700
That’s all I needed to hear just from somebody
I cared about who I knew cared about me telling

00:21:23.700 --> 00:21:25.830
me something like that.”

00:21:25.830 --> 00:21:31.090
You know throughout your career you’re going
to have hundreds of thousands of doubts (even

00:21:31.090 --> 00:21:32.299
while working).

00:21:32.299 --> 00:21:37.410
I spoke to Jack Lemmon on a film that I was
doing…that he was doing and I happened to

00:21:37.410 --> 00:21:39.830
be working with him is what really happened.

00:21:39.830 --> 00:21:48.139
And I asked him…I mean he’s a legend I
knew that then when I was in my 20’s working

00:21:48.139 --> 00:21:49.430
with him.

00:21:49.430 --> 00:21:54.129
And I asked him how he dealt with challenges
back in his day.

00:21:54.129 --> 00:21:58.080
Grandpa was a contract player, so he had worked
every week whether he was getting paid.

00:21:58.080 --> 00:22:02.710
And he was like “Oh, my God, Clifton, I
thought every week was my last gig.

00:22:02.710 --> 00:22:04.279
It’s Jack Lemmon!”&nbsp;

00:22:04.279 --> 00:22:06.620
You thought every gig was your last?

00:22:06.620 --> 00:22:07.620
So I’m okay.

00:22:07.620 --> 00:22:10.519
I’m okay when I get really scared or I think
I’m not going to make it, I’ve got my

00:22:10.519 --> 00:22:14.220
bills paid for the most part, you know I’m
good.

00:22:14.220 --> 00:22:16.309
I don’t live in a mansion but I don’t
want a mansion.

00:22:16.309 --> 00:22:18.809
I don’t need a mansion, not to be happy.

00:22:18.809 --> 00:22:21.100
Film Courage: Problems of prosperity.

00:22:21.100 --> 00:22:29.070
What do you want people to take from your
character Nathan [in A CROOKED SOMEBODY]?

00:22:29.070 --> 00:22:37.399
Clifton: I don’t know…people being more
open-minded to things, questioning things,

00:22:37.399 --> 00:22:43.610
being moved by the film in some way, shape
or form that makes them a better person, hopefully?

00:22:43.610 --> 00:22:46.340
I don’t know, hopefully it’s entertaining?

00:22:46.340 --> 00:22:47.659
Film Courage: It is.

00:22:47.659 --> 00:22:49.529
Clifton: Just be entertained.

00:22:49.529 --> 00:22:50.529
I think everything else will follow.

00:22:50.529 --> 00:22:52.639
It’s a fun one.

00:22:52.639 --> 00:22:56.510
Film Courage: Yes and there’s an interesting
ending.

00:22:56.510 --> 00:22:58.150
I don’t want to spoil it.

00:22:58.150 --> 00:23:00.669
Clifton: So many moments in that film so interesting.

00:23:00.669 --> 00:23:02.740
It’s a fun one.

00:23:02.740 --> 00:23:04.980
I’m happy with this one.

00:23:04.980 --> 00:23:05.980
I’m really happy.

00:23:05.980 --> 00:23:09.340
I’m a tough critic, especially of my own
stuff.

00:23:09.340 --> 00:23:12.169
I’d be the first to tell you to not see
it let me put it that way.

00:23:12.169 --> 00:23:14.840
I’ve had friends who are like “Hey, we’re
going to go watch you.

00:23:14.840 --> 00:23:15.840
We want to support you.”

00:23:15.840 --> 00:23:18.730
I’m like “No, you go see it because you
want to see the movie, don’t go to support

00:23:18.730 --> 00:23:22.749
me because I don’t want to take the blame,”
This is a good one.

00:23:22.749 --> 00:23:25.950
I won’t be taking any blame, it’s a good
one.

